// A test that FAILS on purpose, so a `List.` call over the untyped `[]` has its
// whole failure REPORT compared byte for byte.
//
// # Why this is a separate fixture from untyped_list_calls.nomi
//
// Both corpus sites of this construct are inside an `assert`
// (tests/06-collections/lists_test.nomi:27-28), and an assertion
// subject is not an ordinary expression: `g.traceVar` is set, so binary()
// hoists its right operand and calls recordComparisonOperands, which renders
// BOTH operands into the failure's `values:` rows. `List.head([])` answers the
// bare `None` sentinel, whose kind has no payload type of its own — so
// recordComparisonOperands consults inspector() for tagBareNone, and when no
// arm answers it returns SILENTLY without recording anything. That is the
// failure mode this fixture exists to catch: the answer stays right, the
// report loses two rows, and untyped_list_calls.nomi cannot see it at all.
//
// # Why it has to fail
//
// `tests/` contains no program that fails an assertion, so the corpus never
// checks failure text. The only way to check an
// operand rendering is to produce one. This file is NOT in the corpus and is
// never run by `nomi test tests`; internal/irbuild and internal/vm tests run
// it, and the non-zero exit is part of the recorded golden.
//
// Every failing subject puts the `List.` call on the LEFT and the untyped
// literal on the right, which is not laziness: the report suppresses an operand
// that reads exactly like its value, so `None` and `[]` never produce a row of
// their own, and reversing the order would compare nothing.
// The CALL is what forces the row, and that is the operand the discharge is
// about.
test "an untyped List call that holds" {
    assert List.head([]) == None
    assert List.tail([]) == None
    assert List.concat([], []) == []
    assert List.compare([], []) == Ordering.Equal
}

test "a failing head renders the untyped call" {
    assert List.head([]) != None
}

test "a failing tail renders the untyped call" {
    assert List.tail([]) != None
}

test "a failing concat renders the untyped call" {
    assert List.concat([], []) != []
}

test "a failing compare renders the untyped call" {
    refute List.compare([], []) == Ordering.Equal
}
